      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Investigators at Duke University Medical Center studied the impact of depression on long-term mortality risk in patients with significant coronary artery disease (CAD), defined as > or = 75% narrowing of > or = 1 coronary artery. Participants were classified after testing into nondepressed, mildly depressed and moderately to severely depressed groups. From 5-10 years after the first cardiac catheterization, the mortality ratios were progressively high for the more depressed cohort."},
